## Purpose

Enforce recipient consent/capability checks on every relay first-contact delivery path before durable routing.

## Work

- Require valid recipient-issued authority in production before `R.send` or equivalent durable enqueue accepts first-contact peer mail.
- Preserve Ed25519 sender authentication and alias binding; do not treat sender authentication as recipient consent.
- Apply identical validation to HTTP, WebSocket, connector outbox/sync, direct relay CLI, retries, and any batch/room-adjacent DM path.
- Bind grants to the intended recipient/host and sender where specified; reject guessed, leaked-but-bound-elsewhere, expired, revoked, replayed, malformed, wrong-version, and downgraded authority.
- Return enumeration-resistant errors and avoid writing rejected content to recipient inboxes, dead letters, archives, or content-bearing audit logs.
- Keep rate/size limits as defence in depth after admission, not as substitutes for consent.

## Acceptance evidence

- An authenticated but unauthorised relay peer cannot deliver by knowing or guessing a private alias.
- Deliberate route/capability sharing permits only the intended scope.
- Revocation/expiry/replay take effect under concurrent requests and across restarts.
- Route and backend parity tests prove no bypass path remains.

## B261 post-B219 constraints

Gate direct send, `send_all`, and forwarded local delivery before inbox insertion. Secondary WebSocket/observer/short-queue/stats/dead-letter effects must depend on first accepted atomic admission. Connector filtering remains defence in depth.